{
  "image_name": "01b38a33605db24123e451e48b172aa8.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates two ways enhancers can become active from initially compact chromatin. In both cases, DNA is wrapped around nucleosomes, and activation involves transcription factors binding between nucleosomes and increased histone acetylation, indicated by small yellow circles labeled H3K27ac.\n\nOn the left, enhancer activation is shown as a stepwise developmental process. During cell differentiation, a lineage-determining transcription factor (LDTF) and cell type–specific factors (CTFs) first bind to create a “primed” or “poised” enhancer with low activity. A later external signal then recruits signal-dependent transcription factors (SDTFs), converting this pre-marked region into a fully active enhancer. This panel emphasizes that some enhancers are established in advance and then activated later by signaling.\n\nOn the right, the figure shows a more direct route in which a signal acts on compact chromatin to generate a “signal-dependent de novo enhancer.” Here, the LDTF and SDTFs appear together after stimulation, without an obvious intermediate poised state.\n\nOverall, the comparison highlights two enhancer activation modes: one where developmental factors preconfigure a low-activity enhancer before signaling, and another where signaling creates a new enhancer directly in previously compact chromatin.",
